to mobilize public hostility

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:18.540
against the protestors.

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:22.210
So therefore, and from
there I would proceed

00:05:22.210 --> 00:05:24.640
to the second part of the presentation

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:28.000
where I aim to shed
light on these strategies

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:30.570
by just across two case studies,

00:05:30.570 --> 00:05:33.190
popular pro-democracy protests.

00:05:33.190 --> 00:05:37.400
One is the Four-eight Uprising
in 1988 and the second

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:41.990
is the Saffron Revolution in 2007.

00:05:41.990 --> 00:05:46.990
And based on my analysis of
original qualitative data,

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:57.800
I hope that via this talk I
be able to show first of all,

00:05:59.740 --> 00:06:03.560
a typology of the military
suppressive repertoire,

00:06:03.560 --> 00:06:08.560
as well as to show to the
audience today the various effects

00:06:09.690 --> 00:06:13.150
of these different strategy combinations.

00:06:13.150 --> 00:06:16.370
And before I end the talk,
I will also touch briefly

00:06:16.370 --> 00:06:19.000
on how the findings from my research

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:22.080
would also have relevant implications

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:25.210
for the current situation
in post coup Myanmar

00:06:25.210 --> 00:06:29.510
as well as for the other
situations of suppression

00:06:29.510 --> 00:06:32.990
of the civic space beyond Myanmar as well.

00:06:32.990 --> 00:06:36.240
So first of all, what do we know

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:40.880
in terms of the state repression
of dissent in Myanmar.

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:45.150
According to existing independent reports

00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:49.140
as well as academic studies and research,

00:06:49.140 --> 00:06:52.930
the five decades of military
dictatorship in Myanmar

00:06:52.930 --> 00:06:55.820
were a period of brutal repression

00:06:55.820 --> 00:06:58.330
against pro-democracy activism.

00:06:58.330 --> 00:07:03.330
Like from the 1960s to
the 1970s, to the 19,

00:07:06.100 --> 00:07:10.090
sorry, one second, 1980s, up to the 1990s

00:07:10.090 --> 00:07:12.600
and throughout the 2000s.

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:17.490
So during like these five
decades what we have witnessed

00:07:17.490 --> 00:07:22.490
is how the military rule
has resulted in thousands

00:07:25.970 --> 00:07:30.590
of casualties and deaths of the protestors

00:07:30.590 --> 00:07:33.350
as well as thousands more arrests.

00:07:33.350 --> 00:07:38.350
And these are the very like
quiet well cited numbers

00:07:40.150 --> 00:07:42.770
by the reports by Amnesty International

00:07:42.770 --> 00:07:45.770
as well as reports by
the special repertoires

00:07:45.770 --> 00:07:47.970
on the situation of
human rights in Myanmar.

00:07:49.450 --> 00:07:52.680
In addition, after each
of the protest cycle,

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:55.120
the Burmese military junta is also known

00:07:55.120 --> 00:07:59.550
to have for the confine the
domestic opposition movement

00:07:59.550 --> 00:08:03.300
toward a highly restricted
forum of party politics

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:06.280
that the regime could control.

00:08:06.280 --> 00:08:10.620
So in here, right, like
works by Christina Fink

00:08:10.620 --> 00:08:14.900
and Kyaw Yin Hlaing as well as
Linnea Beatty together offer

00:08:14.900 --> 00:08:17.830
a very informative description
of how the government

00:08:17.830 --> 00:08:20.890
was determined to persecute dissidents.

00:08:20.890 --> 00:08:23.200
So the regime bestow by making all

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:26.560
of the dissident activities
illegal detaining thousands

00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:29.520
of prominent activists and
issuing arrest warrants

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:33.130
for other thousands of activists in exile,

00:08:33.130 --> 00:08:36.590
and on top of that only
allowing political parties

00:08:36.590 --> 00:08:39.863
with no campaigning
activities to be legal.

00:08:41.710 --> 00:08:46.390
And during these five decades,
although less well documented

00:08:46.390 --> 00:08:50.210
the regime leaders they have
also run multiple campaigns

00:08:50.210 --> 00:08:53.170
to vilify the opposition and mobilize

00:08:53.170 --> 00:08:56.010
for public hostility toward protestors.

00:08:56.010 --> 00:09:01.010
So also with a goal, right
to quell dissent yet compared

00:09:01.950 --> 00:09:04.080
to the more direct tools of repression,

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:06.160
such as protests, crackdowns arrests,

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:08.010
and massacres, et cetera.

00:09:08.010 --> 00:09:11.020
The strategy is to
provoke public hostility

00:09:11.020 --> 00:09:12.410
much less analyze.

00:09:12.410 --> 00:09:15.770
And most of the literature
on this issue so far

00:09:15.770 --> 00:09:18.570
in the Myanmar context only draws

00:09:18.570 --> 00:09:23.530
from either public propaganda
by the military in 1988

00:09:23.530 --> 00:09:28.482
or around 1988, or a handful
of activists accounts

00:09:28.482 --> 00:09:33.482
of 1988 protest cycles.

00:09:34.831 --> 00:09:37.095
Okay. So for instance, right.

00:09:37.095 --> 00:09:42.095
So according to Berto Linares
depiction of the military coup

00:09:46.700 --> 00:09:51.390
that ended the Four-eight
Uprising on September 18th, 1988.

00:09:51.390 --> 00:09:54.950
In order to disseminate
allegations of the uprising

00:09:54.950 --> 00:09:59.950
as harboring criminals,
the state broadcasted,

00:10:00.570 --> 00:10:04.050
and also labeled any protests participants

00:10:04.050 --> 00:10:06.130
as violent delinquents.

00:10:06.130 --> 00:10:09.350
And such type of intentional framing

00:10:09.350 --> 00:10:13.900
was also more blatant later
on as state also reported

00:10:13.900 --> 00:10:18.900
about the protestors as quote
unquote destructive elements.

00:10:20.590 --> 00:10:23.770
And at the junta even announced
that most of the deaths

00:10:23.770 --> 00:10:28.220
on the September 18 were
caused by quote looters

00:10:28.220 --> 00:10:31.653
and other unsavory elements unquote.

00:10:32.680 --> 00:10:36.190
Moreover, according to
other studies, right?

00:10:36.190 --> 00:10:38.260
According to a lot of
studies, we also have found

00:10:38.260 --> 00:10:43.260
that after the pro-democracy
uprising in 1988,

00:10:43.950 --> 00:10:46.520
the military regime held a series

00:10:46.520 --> 00:10:50.210
of military press conferences accusing

00:10:50.210 --> 00:10:52.920
the main opposition party at the time,

00:10:52.920 --> 00:10:56.480
the National League for
Democracy, NLD of being controlled

00:10:56.480 --> 00:10:59.933
by the Communist Party of
Burma or foreign forces.

00:11:01.740 --> 00:11:04.563
So, most of these types
of studies and reports

00:11:04.563 --> 00:11:07.460
that we have seen the materials have come

00:11:07.460 --> 00:11:12.340
from the public propaganda
of the military junta.

00:11:12.340 --> 00:11:17.340
However, we do have a glance I like

00:11:19.540 --> 00:11:23.050
the more deceptive strategies via the work

00:11:23.050 --> 00:11:27.660
by Christina Fink where
she quoted the experiences

00:11:27.660 --> 00:11:31.510
of student activists
in 1988, who suspected

00:11:31.510 --> 00:11:34.420
that military Asians in
plain clothes were trying

00:11:34.420 --> 00:11:37.960
to provoke them toward
committing violence or murder

00:11:37.960 --> 00:11:41.370
in order to create a chaotic
situation so that the military

00:11:41.370 --> 00:11:45.290
would have a justification
for clamping down.

00:11:45.290 --> 00:11:50.290
However, overall like one
of these types of studies

00:11:50.340 --> 00:11:54.927
or research and reports have
been able to examine fully

00:11:56.920 --> 00:12:01.700
the range of the various
strategies, both public,

00:12:01.700 --> 00:12:04.410
as well as the covert strategies

00:12:04.410 --> 00:12:09.410
to provoke public hostilities
to work the protest movement

00:12:09.800 --> 00:12:14.800
and moreover, due to the lack of evidence

00:12:15.670 --> 00:12:20.670
from the point of view of the
people on the receiving end

00:12:21.310 --> 00:12:24.860
of these strategies, namely the protestors

00:12:24.860 --> 00:12:29.150
and the general public
themselves, like via these work

00:12:29.150 --> 00:12:31.880
we also have not been
able to really understand

00:12:31.880 --> 00:12:36.880
whether these types of
strategies, have they really like

00:12:37.450 --> 00:12:39.223
how much have they had in effect.

00:12:40.670 --> 00:12:44.560
So therefore this is the
part that I would like

00:12:44.560 --> 00:12:47.350
to contribute with my research.

00:12:47.350 --> 00:12:50.290
And more specifically,
in order to shed light

00:12:50.290 --> 00:12:53.310
on the state leader's accountability

00:12:53.310 --> 00:12:57.280
in manufacturing public hostility
toward protest movements,

00:12:57.280 --> 00:13:02.280
I analyze original firsthand
evidence from two case studies

00:13:02.370 --> 00:13:05.140
of popular pro-democracy protests.

00:13:05.140 --> 00:13:07.770
As I said before, the
Four-eight Uprising in 1988

00:13:07.770 --> 00:13:09.973
and the Saffron Revolution in 2007.

00:13:10.930 --> 00:13:14.670
Not only are they unlikely
cases for the emergence

00:13:14.670 --> 00:13:17.980
of civilian hostility toward
the protest movements,

00:13:17.980 --> 00:13:20.770
but they also experienced
two different combinations

00:13:20.770 --> 00:13:24.430
of safe strategies, which
I would describe later on.

00:13:24.430 --> 00:13:27.210
Therefore I find that
studying the public response

00:13:27.210 --> 00:13:31.170
toward the peaceful
activists in these two cases

00:13:31.170 --> 00:13:33.880
will allow me to highlight
impacts of each type

00:13:33.880 --> 00:13:35.610
of those state repertoire.

00:13:35.610 --> 00:13:40.610
So, first of all, the protest
events in both 1988 and 2007,

00:13:42.630 --> 00:13:45.820
they are unlikely cases
for civilian disruption

00:13:45.820 --> 00:13:48.310
and hostility to where the protestors,

00:13:48.310 --> 00:13:50.400
since these protests were highly popular

00:13:50.400 --> 00:13:53.650
and received widespread approval
from the general public.

00:13:53.650 --> 00:13:56.240
And at the same time, the
antagonism and grievances

00:13:56.240 --> 00:13:59.763
toward the military regime was also high.

00:14:01.270 --> 00:14:04.990
So the Four-eight or
8888 Uprising witnessed

00:14:04.990 --> 00:14:08.280
it's first main protests
on August 8th, 1988

00:14:08.280 --> 00:14:11.020
and the uprising was a
combination of a cycle

00:14:11.020 --> 00:14:14.150
of student activism in Yangon
in the preceding months

00:14:14.150 --> 00:14:15.960
due to the student's grievances

00:14:15.960 --> 00:14:19.440
toward General Ne Win's
one party dictatorship.

00:14:19.440 --> 00:14:24.440
And from mid August onward,
the uprising then became like

00:14:25.410 --> 00:14:28.530
a festival during which
the general public,

00:14:28.530 --> 00:14:32.730
including factory workers,
farmers, office staff, artists,

00:14:32.730 --> 00:14:36.240
homemakers, civil servants
and soldiers throughout

00:14:36.240 --> 00:14:39.100
the country turned out
in droves to demonstrate.

00:14:39.100 --> 00:14:42.900
However, almost one month later,
the military stitcher coup

00:14:42.900 --> 00:14:45.750
and brutally cracked
down on the protesters.

00:14:45.750 --> 00:14:48.903
As I said before, on September 18th, 1988.

00:14:49.790 --> 00:14:53.750
Later on as for the second revolution

00:14:53.750 --> 00:14:57.080
this is a protest cycle that occurred

00:14:57.080 --> 00:15:02.080
during September, 2007
and it earned it's name

00:15:03.080 --> 00:15:05.570
because it was led by
Burmese Buddhist monks

00:15:05.570 --> 00:15:08.750
whose ropes usually have
maroon saffron colors.

00:15:08.750 --> 00:15:12.220
The immediate cause was
government sudden reduction

00:15:12.220 --> 00:15:15.840
in fuel subsidy that led to a
large increase in fuel prices.

00:15:15.840 --> 00:15:19.120
And this brought hardship on
the majority of the population

00:15:19.120 --> 00:15:22.050
who lived near or in poverty.

00:15:22.050 --> 00:15:24.040
As a result of the amounts
they also started to take

00:15:24.040 --> 00:15:26.430
to the streets all over the country.

00:15:26.430 --> 00:15:28.420
Over 10 days after the start

00:15:28.420 --> 00:15:32.260
of the main protest
starting on September 26th,

00:15:32.260 --> 00:15:34.920
the security forces violently cracked down

00:15:34.920 --> 00:15:38.300
on the demonstrators and
raided multiple monasteries

00:15:38.300 --> 00:15:39.340
that night.

00:15:39.340 --> 00:15:42.620
And so consequently, all of
the suffering protests thoughts

00:15:42.620 --> 00:15:44.703
around the end of September.

00:15:45.940 --> 00:15:50.440
But despite the popularity, both 1988

00:15:50.440 --> 00:15:53.880
and 2007 protest events
faced many incidents

00:15:53.880 --> 00:15:57.910
where local residents either
stay away from protest events

00:15:57.910 --> 00:16:01.990
or actively confronted and
blocked protest marches.

00:16:01.990 --> 00:16:06.860
And moreover the military itself

00:16:06.860 --> 00:16:10.880
also employ different
combinations of strategies

00:16:10.880 --> 00:16:13.860
to provoke anti protestor hostility

00:16:13.860 --> 00:16:16.210
and legitimize their repression.

00:16:16.210 --> 00:16:19.870
So at first, as you can
see in this table here

00:16:19.870 --> 00:16:23.530
in both 1988 and 2007 as each protest

00:16:23.530 --> 00:16:26.040
was gaining popularity, the government

00:16:26.040 --> 00:16:29.150
did not crack down right
away, but started broadcasting

00:16:29.150 --> 00:16:32.043
his allegations of
protestors as criminals.

00:16:33.250 --> 00:16:37.340
The regime avoided using the
word protesters to describe

00:16:37.340 --> 00:16:40.740
the movement participants,
but instead labeling them

00:16:40.740 --> 00:16:44.920
as rioters in order to
promote criminal images.

00:16:44.920 --> 00:16:47.290
And at the same time, the state agents,

00:16:47.290 --> 00:16:51.990
they also were part of a covert strategy

00:16:51.990 --> 00:16:55.470
where they disguised as
civilian observers penetrated

00:16:55.470 --> 00:16:59.030
the protest and committed
violence towards security forces

00:16:59.030 --> 00:17:01.030
as well as towards civilian targets,

00:17:01.030 --> 00:17:02.430
such as attacking the police

00:17:02.430 --> 00:17:04.853
and or destroying public properties.

00:17:05.790 --> 00:17:10.790
In addition, in 1988, in
order to manufacture images

00:17:10.960 --> 00:17:14.730
of quote unquote anarchy which is the word

00:17:14.730 --> 00:17:19.390
that the regime later on use
to describe the 1988 events.

00:17:19.390 --> 00:17:22.980
So in order to manufacture
these type of anarchy images

00:17:22.980 --> 00:17:26.550
that would further sow
distinction between protestors

00:17:26.550 --> 00:17:30.480
and the general public and also
to justify a military coup,

00:17:30.480 --> 00:17:34.580
the regime can also covertly
incited peaceful protestors,

00:17:34.580 --> 00:17:37.220
and dissidents to commit violence

00:17:37.220 --> 00:17:38.933
against innocent civilians.

00:17:40.400 --> 00:17:45.400
So in order to further examine,
like the different methods

00:17:47.130 --> 00:17:49.180
of these types of strategies

00:17:49.180 --> 00:17:53.540
as well as to examine
like their varied effects,

00:17:53.540 --> 00:17:57.630
I would now turn to
describing to you the data

00:17:57.630 --> 00:18:00.950
that I have collected as
well as how I'm planning

00:18:00.950 --> 00:18:03.080
to analyze them as well.

00:18:03.080 --> 00:18:05.970
So during my field work,

00:18:05.970 --> 00:18:10.570
I conducted in-person
semi-structured interviews

00:18:10.570 --> 00:18:14.720
and collected written accounts
mostly by Yangon residents

00:18:14.720 --> 00:18:17.920
from diverse demographic
and political backgrounds.

00:18:17.920 --> 00:18:22.660
And since I try to mirror the
geographical representation

00:18:22.660 --> 00:18:26.280
of Yangon residents in my
sample, I can also take advantage

00:18:26.280 --> 00:18:29.820
of the largely accidental
exposure and non exposure

00:18:29.820 --> 00:18:32.940
to safe strategies among the interviewees

00:18:32.940 --> 00:18:36.490
in order to highlight
the strategy's effects

00:18:36.490 --> 00:18:38.143
on civilian responses.

00:18:39.320 --> 00:18:42.700
And in order to build
trust with the interviewees

00:18:42.700 --> 00:18:45.030
while maximizing the
diversity of the sample

00:18:46.722 --> 00:18:49.410
I also worked with
private schools, libraries

00:18:49.410 --> 00:18:53.070
and institutions in Yangon and
as well as state affiliates

00:18:53.070 --> 00:18:55.560
to recruit respondents.

00:18:55.560 --> 00:19:00.010
The interview process
stopped once my sample reach,

00:19:00.010 --> 00:19:02.910
like all of the sampling criteria,

00:19:02.910 --> 00:19:05.530
as you can see in here
regarding the demographic

00:19:05.530 --> 00:19:08.320
as well as political representation

00:19:08.320 --> 00:19:11.810
and when the data also reached saturation,

00:19:11.810 --> 00:19:15.030
which means, when the new interview data

00:19:15.030 --> 00:19:17.060
I was mostly redundant and also offer

00:19:17.060 --> 00:19:19.380
too little new information.

00:19:19.380 --> 00:19:22.850
For the retinal towns, I collaborated

00:19:22.850 --> 00:19:25.210
with the same institutions to identify

00:19:25.210 --> 00:19:29.180
and collect published
autobiographies, memoirs

00:19:29.180 --> 00:19:33.540
and interviews by former
activists during these two decades

00:19:33.540 --> 00:19:36.550
between 1988 and 2010 as well.

00:19:36.550 --> 00:19:41.550
So with these graphs here, you
can also basically take plans

00:19:41.790 --> 00:19:46.790
at the distribution of the
different types of demographics,

00:19:46.800 --> 00:19:51.353
like among the respondents
and among the people

00:19:52.620 --> 00:19:55.763
whose written accounts
that I have collected here.

00:19:58.440 --> 00:20:03.030
Right, so based on my analysis
of these types of data,

00:20:06.540 --> 00:20:08.490
what have I found?

00:20:08.490 --> 00:20:11.260
So, first of all, regarding
the state strategies

00:20:11.260 --> 00:20:16.260
to frame protestors for
violence during both the 1988

00:20:17.590 --> 00:20:21.730
and 2007 events, many
interviewees recalled

00:20:21.730 --> 00:20:25.660
how the government spread
news through mass media

00:20:25.660 --> 00:20:29.690
that rioters hit them on mount
and lay men demonstrators

00:20:29.690 --> 00:20:33.430
in order to rob properties,
poison food and water,

00:20:33.430 --> 00:20:36.810
burned down cars and destroy
traffic lights, offices

00:20:36.810 --> 00:20:38.310
and households.

00:20:38.310 --> 00:20:41.540
By broadcasting about
multiple communal unrest

00:20:41.540 --> 00:20:45.260
across different townships in
Yangon the authorities wanted

00:20:45.260 --> 00:20:48.240
to make the public perceive
an immediate threat

00:20:48.240 --> 00:20:53.240
from quote unquote saboteurs
mixing among protestors,

00:20:53.260 --> 00:20:56.330
and these types of news
reached many neighborhoods.

00:20:56.330 --> 00:20:59.770
And as a result, most of my
interviewees heard these rumors,

00:20:59.770 --> 00:21:03.600
even though they might not
have actually witnessed

00:21:03.600 --> 00:21:06.163
the alleged sabotage, right?

00:21:07.260 --> 00:21:11.820
So this is one example here.

00:21:11.820 --> 00:21:14.250
And at the same time in 1988,

00:21:15.530 --> 00:21:20.410
the regime also shut down
government offices for weeks

00:21:20.410 --> 00:21:23.730
and also blamed the
protestors for the shutdown,

00:21:23.730 --> 00:21:26.233
charging them with
vandalizing these offices.

00:21:27.180 --> 00:21:30.730
So in the next quote
here, what you'll find

00:21:30.730 --> 00:21:34.940
is that this is a former
civil servant who recalled

00:21:34.940 --> 00:21:37.260
how she received quote unquote news

00:21:38.280 --> 00:21:42.363
about her offices destruction in advance.

00:21:43.260 --> 00:21:44.360
Yeah.

00:21:44.360 --> 00:21:49.360
And secondly, in order to further produce

00:21:50.370 --> 00:21:54.300
the optical illusions of
protestors as criminals,

00:21:54.300 --> 00:21:56.130
the state they use their own Asians

00:21:56.130 --> 00:21:59.760
to infiltrate peaceful
protests and instigate riots.

00:21:59.760 --> 00:22:03.850
In 1988, the state Asians committed arsons

00:22:03.850 --> 00:22:06.370
and carry out robberies
in different townships

00:22:06.370 --> 00:22:09.900
so as to make local residents
believe in the allegations.

00:22:09.900 --> 00:22:14.900
And in 2007, these Asians also
attack the security forces

00:22:16.160 --> 00:22:20.150
with bricks and rocks so that
the military could use this

00:22:20.150 --> 00:22:22.850
as a justification for soldiers

00:22:22.850 --> 00:22:24.853
to shoot protesters later on.

00:22:25.820 --> 00:22:29.200
And as you can see from these quotes here,

00:22:29.200 --> 00:22:32.470
multiple protests,
participants and observers

00:22:32.470 --> 00:22:35.303
have detailed these types
of accounts as well.

00:22:37.740 --> 00:22:42.740
Right. But not stopping there, right?

00:22:43.940 --> 00:22:48.940
So at the height of the
Four-eight Uprising,

00:22:50.940 --> 00:22:54.860
the state itself was also
involved in inciting protester

00:22:54.860 --> 00:22:58.880
toward committing
violence toward civilians.

00:22:58.880 --> 00:23:00.143
So what happens?

00:23:01.010 --> 00:23:03.560
At the height of the
Four-eight Uprising the states

00:23:05.760 --> 00:23:09.420
they released prisoners and
also had these prisoners poison

00:23:09.420 --> 00:23:12.700
the public water sources
that the protestors drank

00:23:13.972 --> 00:23:15.650
and on top of that,
they also hired a people

00:23:15.650 --> 00:23:17.690
to attack these activist as well.

00:23:17.690 --> 00:23:21.260
And multiple residents in
Yangon witnessed these attempts.

00:23:21.260 --> 00:23:26.260
However, as the Rangoon general
strike committee at the time

00:23:26.300 --> 00:23:29.550
was worried about the
government scheme to frame

00:23:29.550 --> 00:23:34.150
the people's uprising as
chaotic and as anarchic,

00:23:34.150 --> 00:23:37.350
they released a public
statement requesting people

00:23:37.350 --> 00:23:41.780
not to harm and not to
kill the alleged criminals.

00:23:41.780 --> 00:23:45.660
Former student activists in Yangon in 1988

00:23:45.660 --> 00:23:49.170
likewise here also recall
how they were involved

00:23:49.170 --> 00:23:51.110
in detaining the alleged criminals,

00:23:51.110 --> 00:23:53.223
but did not condone killing them.

00:23:54.980 --> 00:23:59.980
And in another example provided
by the written accounts

00:24:01.160 --> 00:24:06.160
by a strike committee member himself,

00:24:06.280 --> 00:24:08.470
after the strike committee caught a monk

00:24:08.470 --> 00:24:12.763
with three Legion police
which were homemade weapons.

00:24:14.556 --> 00:24:17.100
The committee just interrogated this monk,

00:24:17.100 --> 00:24:20.253
confiscated the gingerly,
and then let the monk go.

00:24:21.650 --> 00:24:23.863
So overall, right?

00:24:24.850 --> 00:24:26.960
The general strike committee at that time,

00:24:26.960 --> 00:24:30.140
they also try very hard to make
sure that they could control

00:24:30.140 --> 00:24:32.530
these different protesters
and strike groups

00:24:32.530 --> 00:24:34.870
and strike committees so as to make sure

00:24:34.870 --> 00:24:39.870
that do not commit violence
against innocent civilians.

00:24:42.420 --> 00:24:46.020
Therefore, according to
many activists and observers

00:24:46.020 --> 00:24:50.610
in 1988, in order to incite
protesters toward suspecting

00:24:50.610 --> 00:24:53.160
and also killing innocent civilians,

00:24:53.160 --> 00:24:57.340
the military resorted to
using agents provocateurs.

00:24:57.340 --> 00:25:00.090
So first, in cases

00:25:00.090 --> 00:25:04.000
where the strike committee's
petro alleged criminals,

00:25:04.000 --> 00:25:08.600
the state agents, they infiltrated
the public interrogations

00:25:08.600 --> 00:25:12.940
and also provoked public
anger, which drove people

00:25:12.940 --> 00:25:15.060
to behave those that they had arrested

00:25:15.060 --> 00:25:17.533
without concrete evidence of crimes.

00:25:18.420 --> 00:25:23.113
And one Yangon residents
described such intervention here.

00:25:25.850 --> 00:25:27.740
And many other witnesses.

00:25:27.740 --> 00:25:30.810
They also corroborate the
presence of state Asians

00:25:30.810 --> 00:25:34.860
in inciting brutal punishment
of captured criminal suspects

00:25:34.860 --> 00:25:36.210
in similar cases as well.

00:25:36.210 --> 00:25:39.153
And this is just one of those quotes.

00:25:40.470 --> 00:25:43.860
And immediately afterwards the
government they also arrested

00:25:43.860 --> 00:25:48.390
the strike committee
members to whom Asians

00:25:48.390 --> 00:25:50.480
had earlier provoked.

00:25:50.480 --> 00:25:55.480
And finally the state
agents at the same time,

00:25:56.220 --> 00:25:59.430
they photographed and
filmed these incidents

00:25:59.430 --> 00:26:01.730
of public executions and broadcast them

00:26:01.730 --> 00:26:03.603
as evidence of the uprising's NRP.

00:26:09.020 --> 00:26:13.640
The state's next strategy
with dropping protesters

00:26:13.640 --> 00:26:14.850
and strike committee members

00:26:14.850 --> 00:26:17.610
in order to make them
vulnerable to manipulation

00:26:17.610 --> 00:26:20.650
into publicly executing innocent people.

00:26:20.650 --> 00:26:23.660
Not only did they drop
people, but in many cases,

00:26:23.660 --> 00:26:26.470
they even arrested these
protestors later on

00:26:26.470 --> 00:26:30.053
in order to cover up like
their involvement as well.

00:26:30.970 --> 00:26:34.830
So here is a quote by a resident

00:26:34.830 --> 00:26:37.350
in the town of Mawlamyine who recalled

00:26:37.350 --> 00:26:41.573
how one of her acquaintances
also fell into this trap.

00:26:42.940 --> 00:26:47.940
And last but not least state
Asians they provided guns

00:26:48.290 --> 00:26:51.630
and homemade weapons to
neighborhood strike committees

00:26:51.630 --> 00:26:53.990
in order to facilitate the violent attacks

00:26:53.990 --> 00:26:58.990
and later on also arrested or
executed these people as well.

00:26:59.100 --> 00:27:03.490
So a former student activist
here in 1988 describes

00:27:03.490 --> 00:27:07.603
how her adopted father was framed here.

00:27:10.190 --> 00:27:15.190
And another student union member
in the town of Yenangyaung

00:27:19.720 --> 00:27:23.550
whose name is Maung Maung Wun,
he also met the same fate.

00:27:23.550 --> 00:27:25.290
So at the height of the Four-eight,

00:27:25.290 --> 00:27:27.960
the police in Yenangyaung offered to work

00:27:27.960 --> 00:27:31.520
with the student union to take
care of the city's security.

00:27:31.520 --> 00:27:35.520
The police chief himself also
gave Maung Maung Wun a gun

00:27:35.520 --> 00:27:38.470
in the name of equipping the student union

00:27:38.470 --> 00:27:40.520
to provide law and order.

00:27:40.520 --> 00:27:44.050
And Maung Maung Wun as a
result was later accused

00:27:44.050 --> 00:27:47.193
of stealing this gun and also
was jailed for two years.

00:27:49.070 --> 00:27:54.070
So overall, right like I
just wanted to review here

00:27:54.740 --> 00:27:59.740
in terms of the two different
types of strategy combinations

00:28:01.890 --> 00:28:05.020
that the military they employed

00:28:05.020 --> 00:28:08.450
in 1988 versus in 2007, right?

00:28:08.450 --> 00:28:12.230
So in 2007, whereas most of
the strategies like focused

00:28:12.230 --> 00:28:17.230
on framing protesters for
violence towards civilians

00:28:17.380 --> 00:28:22.380
in 1988, they complimented
these types of strategies

00:28:23.870 --> 00:28:28.160
with the incitement to work mob violence

00:28:28.160 --> 00:28:32.453
against the innocent civilians as well.

00:28:34.260 --> 00:28:37.420
And these states in terms of the effect

00:28:37.420 --> 00:28:40.240
of these types of strategies
on the public perception

00:28:40.240 --> 00:28:43.790
of the protest, these strategies

00:28:43.790 --> 00:28:47.530
they made local residents
consider every protestors,

00:28:47.530 --> 00:28:49.670
a potential threat, right?

00:28:49.670 --> 00:28:51.920
Even in cases where people suspected

00:28:51.920 --> 00:28:55.740
that the government
like had a role to play

00:28:55.740 --> 00:29:00.280
in these types of incidents
and they suspected

00:29:00.280 --> 00:29:02.440
the covert operation by the government,

00:29:02.440 --> 00:29:05.300
but many of the people that I interview

00:29:05.300 --> 00:29:08.810
they still became wary of the
agents provocateurs hiding

00:29:08.810 --> 00:29:12.543
among protest group as you
can see in this quote here.

00:29:13.520 --> 00:29:16.840
And moreover in 1988, right?

00:29:16.840 --> 00:29:20.430
Like many people were also
legitimately frightened

00:29:20.430 --> 00:29:23.680
by the killings and the
headings of civilians.

00:29:23.680 --> 00:29:27.290
A resident of South
Oakleigh township in 1988

00:29:27.290 --> 00:29:31.363
in this quote here shares
her traumatic memory.

00:29:35.800 --> 00:29:40.800
And an interviewee who actually
also lives outside Yangon

00:29:41.580 --> 00:29:46.580
in 1988, also knew about
these killings here,

00:29:46.980 --> 00:29:51.980
the state plan TV broadcast
and therefore he himself

00:29:54.090 --> 00:29:59.090
was also negatively affected
by this type of news as well.

00:29:59.640 --> 00:30:04.270
So therefore, as you can see
people in general did perceive

00:30:04.270 --> 00:30:07.700
a certain level of threats
from the protestors

00:30:07.700 --> 00:30:09.670
and from the dissidents.

00:30:09.670 --> 00:30:14.515
And depending on the level
of threat perception,

00:30:14.515 --> 00:30:19.515
as well as depending on
the members of the public,

00:30:20.420 --> 00:30:24.293
their social and gender expectations.

00:30:25.140 --> 00:30:27.610
What I find is that
these protests observers

00:30:27.610 --> 00:30:31.820
they either passively
avoided protest events

00:30:31.820 --> 00:30:34.730
or withdrew their support to protestors

00:30:34.730 --> 00:30:37.430
or actively blocked protest marches

00:30:37.430 --> 00:30:39.203
and confronted protestors.

00:30:41.846 --> 00:30:44.910
So first of all, regarding
the passive hostility,

00:30:44.910 --> 00:30:48.220
so what I find is the things,
the direct confrontation

00:30:50.377 --> 00:30:55.090
was perceived by many
people as a high risk act

00:30:55.090 --> 00:30:57.790
where people might have
to engage in violently

00:30:57.790 --> 00:30:59.200
with the protestors, right?

00:30:59.200 --> 00:31:01.370
That could result in casualties.

00:31:01.370 --> 00:31:04.960
Most peoples tend to stay away
from crowds that were elected

00:31:04.960 --> 00:31:09.960
to harbor saboteurs, or
criminals or rioters.

00:31:10.420 --> 00:31:15.420
And as a result in 1988, people
would rather stay at home

00:31:15.670 --> 00:31:18.410
and guard their own
places instead of going

00:31:18.410 --> 00:31:20.800
outside to support protests.

00:31:20.800 --> 00:31:25.130
And similarly, since the
beginning of the 2007 events

00:31:25.130 --> 00:31:29.230
due to fear criminals
mixing among the protesters,

00:31:29.230 --> 00:31:32.760
many interviewees they also
stayed away from protest areas

00:31:32.760 --> 00:31:34.770
and avoided traveling out of town

00:31:34.770 --> 00:31:37.193
as it was considered unsafe.

00:31:39.220 --> 00:31:40.340
Moreover, right?

00:31:40.340 --> 00:31:44.370
So regarding active
facility, according to sorry,

00:31:44.370 --> 00:31:45.203
before I go into that.

00:31:45.203 --> 00:31:48.140
So according to my interviewees,
most of the bystanders

00:31:52.563 --> 00:31:54.440
they chose not to
intervene when the military

00:31:55.280 --> 00:31:59.670
or the police dealt with the
accused quote, unquote rioters

00:31:59.670 --> 00:32:03.233
or criminals in 1988 or in 2007.

00:32:04.460 --> 00:32:08.150
As people consider these
protesters as threat,

00:32:08.150 --> 00:32:09.700
they became reluctant to support

00:32:09.700 --> 00:32:12.360
or help during government crack downs.

00:32:12.360 --> 00:32:13.680
And furthermore, right?

00:32:13.680 --> 00:32:17.860
Like in the later days of
the Four-eight Uprising

00:32:17.860 --> 00:32:21.640
due to a high level of
threat reception adult men

00:32:21.640 --> 00:32:25.890
from various neighborhoods they
also became morally applies

00:32:25.890 --> 00:32:29.470
to secure their own the streets
and confront protestors.

00:32:29.470 --> 00:32:32.540
And in a matter of
days, Yangon transformed

00:32:32.540 --> 00:32:35.110
from a friendly and peaceful community

00:32:35.110 --> 00:32:38.760
into a hostile environment
for public demonstrations

00:32:38.760 --> 00:32:41.593
as many of my interviewees recall here.

00:32:42.800 --> 00:32:47.800
And these type of
neighborhoods self-organized,

00:32:48.670 --> 00:32:51.660
vigilante teams, they
also armed themselves

00:32:51.660 --> 00:32:56.150
and blocked protests from
passing through the areas

00:32:56.150 --> 00:32:59.000
in order to avoid a risk
of violence directed

00:32:59.000 --> 00:33:01.230
at their families and their neighbors

00:33:01.230 --> 00:33:03.383
as you can see in these two quotes.

00:33:05.370 --> 00:33:09.893
And due to these forms
of active hostility,

00:33:10.950 --> 00:33:15.513
it has a direct effect on the
pro-democracy movement itself.

00:33:16.660 --> 00:33:18.480
And here in this quote you can see

00:33:18.480 --> 00:33:23.250
a former student activists
describing the difficulty

00:33:23.250 --> 00:33:26.000
of carrying out public
protests at the time

00:33:26.000 --> 00:33:28.760
due to the neighborhood
vigilantes like blocking

00:33:28.760 --> 00:33:29.633
the streets.

00:33:31.240 --> 00:33:36.240
And moreover right, another
former student activists

00:33:38.380 --> 00:33:42.570
also commented on how the state strategies

00:33:42.570 --> 00:33:45.620
they generated a widespread
dissension and also led

00:33:45.620 --> 00:33:50.620
to separation between the general public

00:33:50.890 --> 00:33:55.890
and the student protesters,
which according to him

00:33:56.450 --> 00:34:00.470
was one of the main reasons
why the protest failed

00:34:00.470 --> 00:34:01.420
in the end in 1988.

00:34:03.547 --> 00:34:04.600
All right.

00:34:04.600 --> 00:34:09.600
So in the next section, I would like to go

00:34:09.780 --> 00:34:14.510
through the alternative
explanations for these phenomenons.

00:34:14.510 --> 00:34:16.180
I'm sure that some of you

00:34:16.180 --> 00:34:18.580
might also have those
questions in mind as well.

00:34:19.640 --> 00:34:24.640
So first of all, the first
alternative explanation

00:34:25.950 --> 00:34:28.460
is regarding the government threat,

00:34:28.460 --> 00:34:31.020
sorry, regarding government reward, right?

00:34:31.020 --> 00:34:34.410
So where the disruptive
and hostile civilians,

00:34:34.410 --> 00:34:39.410
like mostly people who received
rewards from the regime.

00:34:41.300 --> 00:34:44.670
So from many of the existing
studies as well as reports,

00:34:44.670 --> 00:34:49.670
we know that state-hired violent
Asians have been involved

00:34:50.440 --> 00:34:53.470
in attacking pro-democracy
activists in Myanmar, right?

00:34:53.470 --> 00:34:57.570
So for example, a
student activist in 1988,

00:34:57.570 --> 00:34:59.870
they are among my
interviewees also remembers

00:34:59.870 --> 00:35:04.310
how the government appointed
administrators in his own word

00:35:04.310 --> 00:35:07.890
who clearly were regime
supporters requested informers

00:35:07.890 --> 00:35:11.270
to come watch and arrest
activists in his area.

00:35:11.270 --> 00:35:15.980
And in 2007, another witness
who lived near the protest side

00:35:15.980 --> 00:35:20.080
also recalled how the
state-hired ducks came to crush

00:35:20.080 --> 00:35:21.483
the protesters as well.

00:35:22.700 --> 00:35:27.270
And other studies have also
shown, right, like based

00:35:27.270 --> 00:35:31.430
on the information and
evidence that they have gather

00:35:31.430 --> 00:35:33.720
they have also described how in some cases

00:35:33.720 --> 00:35:38.720
of the civilian disruption in
1988, that people were hired

00:35:38.750 --> 00:35:41.663
and trained to attack
and kill the protesters.

00:35:42.910 --> 00:35:45.570
So, however, since the
Burmese dictatorship,

00:35:45.570 --> 00:35:48.480
since they limited the number
of the reward recipients

00:35:48.480 --> 00:35:51.740
and the hired agents, even
though these people were involved

00:35:51.740 --> 00:35:54.510
in any type of protest or
violence, they did not make up

00:35:54.510 --> 00:35:56.410
the majority of the cases.

00:35:56.410 --> 00:36:00.050
And indeed like most of
the civilians who disrupted

00:36:00.050 --> 00:36:03.130
the activists or who became
hostile to where the activists,

00:36:03.130 --> 00:36:05.650
as I find from my own interview data,

00:36:05.650 --> 00:36:09.020
they do not receive rewards
prior to the actions.

00:36:09.020 --> 00:36:12.180
And in fact, like most of
the urban Burmese population

00:36:12.180 --> 00:36:17.180
at that time were burdened,
like by poor public services

00:36:17.420 --> 00:36:19.040
and lack of social security,

00:36:19.040 --> 00:36:20.970
which made them become dissatisfied

00:36:20.970 --> 00:36:23.070
with the regime at the first place.

00:36:23.070 --> 00:36:25.840
And therefore a civilian
hostility in my case studies

00:36:25.840 --> 00:36:29.850
was mostly not motivated
by the regime's work.

00:36:29.850 --> 00:36:32.470
And how about the
government's threats, right?

00:36:32.470 --> 00:36:35.610
So where members of the
general public threatened

00:36:35.610 --> 00:36:39.150
to cooperate with the
government crackdown leading

00:36:39.150 --> 00:36:42.903
to hostility and disruption
toward the protesters.

00:36:44.270 --> 00:36:46.360
Even though the security forces

00:36:46.360 --> 00:36:51.360
they didn't threaten
civilians in terms of, sorry,

00:36:54.580 --> 00:36:56.090
even though the security forces state,

00:36:56.090 --> 00:36:58.110
they did threaten the civilians.

00:36:58.110 --> 00:37:00.530
I find that actually this did not have

00:37:00.530 --> 00:37:04.030
any significant effect in
terms of the civilian hostility

00:37:04.030 --> 00:37:08.370
in the cases of 1988 and 2007 events.

00:37:08.370 --> 00:37:10.480
So first of all, people
might have been threatened

00:37:10.480 --> 00:37:13.240
to stay away from protest
sites or not to support

00:37:13.240 --> 00:37:17.160
the protest but there was no
evidence that they were forced

00:37:17.160 --> 00:37:20.950
to actively confront or to
actively block the activists.

00:37:20.950 --> 00:37:25.950
And second, among the people
that I have interview,

00:37:27.950 --> 00:37:31.260
if they themselves have
experienced like these types

00:37:31.260 --> 00:37:33.970
of threats, like from the
military, from the soldiers,

00:37:33.970 --> 00:37:37.240
from the police, telling
them, requesting them, right,

00:37:37.240 --> 00:37:41.910
like not to go out to support
or to watch the protest event

00:37:43.400 --> 00:37:47.110
they would detail those
kinds of experiences

00:37:48.180 --> 00:37:49.330
very clearly, right?

00:37:49.330 --> 00:37:52.660
But at the same time, like, as
they were talking about that,

00:37:52.660 --> 00:37:54.070
they also express, right,

00:37:54.070 --> 00:37:59.070
like how their own traumatic
impression of the rumors

00:38:01.300 --> 00:38:05.020
about violence or the actual
violence that the protestors

00:38:05.020 --> 00:38:10.020
and the dissidents had carry
out also had made them become

00:38:12.280 --> 00:38:17.280
more reluctant as well as
feeling more threatened

00:38:17.710 --> 00:38:20.670
and also becoming more hostile toward

00:38:20.670 --> 00:38:23.160
these protesters as well.

00:38:23.160 --> 00:38:25.410
So those two things,
they could also happen

00:38:25.410 --> 00:38:27.920
at the same time and the interviewees

00:38:28.870 --> 00:38:32.550
they were readily shared about
those types of experiences.

00:38:32.550 --> 00:38:35.670
And secondly, like many
interviewees they also detailed

00:38:35.670 --> 00:38:40.030
how their neighbors and
their mosque pagodas

00:38:40.030 --> 00:38:42.740
and churches even refuse to comply

00:38:42.740 --> 00:38:44.320
with the government's order.

00:38:44.320 --> 00:38:46.960
And many of them also offer shelter

00:38:46.960 --> 00:38:48.663
to the movement participants.

00:38:49.620 --> 00:38:52.750
So therefore the government
press were mostly irrelevant

00:38:52.750 --> 00:38:54.960
in these case studies in terms of shaping

00:38:54.960 --> 00:38:58.950
the civilian hostility and disruption.

00:38:58.950 --> 00:39:01.910
And what about in terms of
ethnic difference, right?

00:39:01.910 --> 00:39:04.880
So in this case, as Burma
Buddhists were thought

00:39:04.880 --> 00:39:07.490
to make up, I mean, they
make up the vast majority

00:39:07.490 --> 00:39:10.040
of the population and therefore
they also were thought

00:39:10.040 --> 00:39:11.500
to make out the vast majority

00:39:11.500 --> 00:39:14.270
of the urban pro-democracy protestors.

00:39:14.270 --> 00:39:17.790
So in this case where non
Burma, like more likely

00:39:17.790 --> 00:39:21.617
to engage in disruption
and the hostilities

00:39:21.617 --> 00:39:23.183
toward the protesters.

00:39:24.430 --> 00:39:29.110
So based on the data
that I have collected,

00:39:29.110 --> 00:39:34.110
there are cases where the
existing ethnic tension hindered

00:39:34.170 --> 00:39:36.570
a non-Burma from supporting the activists.

00:39:36.570 --> 00:39:39.220
So for instance, a Karen men

00:39:39.220 --> 00:39:43.540
who is an ethnic minority
living in Yangon in 2007,

00:39:43.540 --> 00:39:44.528
he shared how his current neighborhood

00:39:44.528 --> 00:39:49.528
was not really bothered
much by the state repression

00:39:49.610 --> 00:39:51.980
of the ethnic Burma monks and protestors.

00:39:51.980 --> 00:39:54.890
And this is because many
ethnic minority populations

00:39:56.570 --> 00:40:01.570
they regarded Burma people as
their oppressors for decades.

00:40:01.800 --> 00:40:04.660
Therefore the protester's
ethnicity background

00:40:04.660 --> 00:40:08.500
also made them appear as
a quote unquote villains

00:40:08.500 --> 00:40:12.620
to these minority neighborhoods
and also deter the support

00:40:12.620 --> 00:40:16.523
from these neighborhoods who
were the protesters as well.

00:40:17.550 --> 00:40:21.590
However, like even though,
like this is one instance

00:40:21.590 --> 00:40:24.540
that I have found like
during the interview,

00:40:24.540 --> 00:40:28.540
but other evidence that
I have like collected

00:40:29.460 --> 00:40:33.373
via my fieldwork have
also shown that observers

00:40:35.200 --> 00:40:37.390
who were Burma Buddhist themselves,

00:40:37.390 --> 00:40:40.790
they were also engaged, right?

00:40:40.790 --> 00:40:44.070
Like in these forms of
hostility and these forms

00:40:44.070 --> 00:40:47.520
of disruption due to
the impression like all

00:40:47.520 --> 00:40:52.520
of the protestors as a
dangerous, a physical threat

00:40:52.620 --> 00:40:53.913
to their neighborhood.

00:40:56.845 --> 00:40:58.770
So, overall, right?

00:40:58.770 --> 00:41:03.770
I also think that these
findings from my research

00:41:04.520 --> 00:41:07.900
and they also have relevant implications

00:41:07.900 --> 00:41:10.980
for the current case of
the post-school Myanmar

00:41:10.980 --> 00:41:12.813
in 2021 as well.

00:41:13.670 --> 00:41:17.060
So from most of the
existing reports and studies

00:41:17.060 --> 00:41:20.440
that we have seen, that
(indistinct) recently,

00:41:20.440 --> 00:41:25.440
they have also used by these
similar types of strategies,

00:41:25.540 --> 00:41:30.020
both employing public, as
well as covert propaganda

00:41:30.020 --> 00:41:33.510
to manipulate narratives about
protesters and frame them

00:41:33.510 --> 00:41:36.220
as criminals in order
to turn public opinion

00:41:36.220 --> 00:41:38.490
against the resistance and in favor

00:41:38.490 --> 00:41:40.090
of state repression, right?

00:41:40.090 --> 00:41:44.220
So with regard to public
propaganda to frame protestors

00:41:44.220 --> 00:41:47.300
of conducting violence
against public interests,

00:41:47.300 --> 00:41:51.330
for example, the military
control Myanmar TV,

00:41:51.330 --> 00:41:54.830
they recently aired an interview
with a monk who claimed

00:41:54.830 --> 00:41:59.180
that NLD supporters burn down
a village and also compare NLD

00:41:59.180 --> 00:42:01.180
to the Taliban, right?

00:42:01.180 --> 00:42:05.010
Which is essentially
associating NLD members

00:42:05.010 --> 00:42:07.640
with criminal images that would resonate

00:42:07.640 --> 00:42:10.190
with the majority of
Burmese Buddhist audience

00:42:10.190 --> 00:42:12.800
many of whom might also
have discrimination

00:42:12.800 --> 00:42:14.990
against people of Muslim heritage.

00:42:14.990 --> 00:42:18.800
And in a similar vein in just last June,

00:42:18.800 --> 00:42:21.970
the state media report they also accused

00:42:21.970 --> 00:42:26.320
the national unity government
and other resistance forces

00:42:26.320 --> 00:42:31.320
of quote, communicating with
international terrorists

00:42:31.810 --> 00:42:35.840
and also insurgent group to grasp

00:42:35.840 --> 00:42:38.173
the state power by force unquote.

00:42:39.210 --> 00:42:42.560
Moreover, the military controlled
Ministry of Information,

00:42:42.560 --> 00:42:44.630
they have also started publishing books

00:42:44.630 --> 00:42:47.490
that accused anti-religion
movement of being

00:42:47.490 --> 00:42:51.083
a quote unquote, a revolution
to bring back vote cheaters.

00:42:51.940 --> 00:42:54.280
The military also disseminated newsletters

00:42:54.280 --> 00:42:58.390
that labeled protests
as rioters as anarctic

00:42:58.390 --> 00:43:01.090
and as sabotage activity.

00:43:01.090 --> 00:43:03.450
So very similar types of rhetorics

00:43:03.450 --> 00:43:08.450
that we have witnessed back
in before 2010 as well.

00:43:09.810 --> 00:43:14.440
On top of that, regarding
with covert strategies,

00:43:14.440 --> 00:43:19.200
the tatmadaw recently, they
have also have mastered

00:43:19.200 --> 00:43:21.550
in their social media repertoire

00:43:21.550 --> 00:43:24.080
and therefore they have
spread online disinformation

00:43:24.080 --> 00:43:29.080
under the guise of ordinary
observers in order to build

00:43:29.310 --> 00:43:32.980
an echo chamber, right effect
a echo chamber reinforcing

00:43:32.980 --> 00:43:34.750
their official propaganda.

00:43:34.750 --> 00:43:38.020
And numerous reports have
found that state court chose

00:43:38.020 --> 00:43:40.750
to have engaged in
disseminating this information

00:43:40.750 --> 00:43:44.540
about anti-government protesters
with false accusations

00:43:44.540 --> 00:43:46.850
and manipulated images of violence.

00:43:46.850 --> 00:43:51.150
So for example, as you can
see, in these kinds of images

00:43:51.150 --> 00:43:55.050
on social media, the pro
military social media accounts,

00:43:55.050 --> 00:43:58.470
they claim the security
forces did not kill protesters

00:43:58.470 --> 00:44:02.260
but it was a third party that
was responsible referring

00:44:02.260 --> 00:44:04.770
to other protestors and other members

00:44:04.770 --> 00:44:07.410
of different resistance forces.

00:44:07.410 --> 00:44:09.990
And some protestors were
even falsely accused

00:44:09.990 --> 00:44:12.740
of killing the police or raping women.

00:44:12.740 --> 00:44:15.320
Other pro-military accounts

00:44:15.320 --> 00:44:18.360
have also accused anti-regime
dissidents who have turned

00:44:18.360 --> 00:44:22.180
to violent methods of being
manipulated by foreign forces

00:44:22.180 --> 00:44:25.590
and Islamist groups like
the ISIS and the Taliban,

00:44:25.590 --> 00:44:28.210
and even suggest without enough evidence

00:44:28.210 --> 00:44:30.943
that Muslims were behind the attacks.

00:44:32.180 --> 00:44:33.013
Yeah.

00:44:33.013 --> 00:44:38.013
So not only in the post-Myanmar in 2020,

00:44:38.190 --> 00:44:43.130
we would also find very
similar and familiar types

00:44:43.130 --> 00:44:47.583
of strategies in other cases,
beyond Myanmar as well.

00:44:48.530 --> 00:44:53.420
So based on my own survey
of the existing literature

00:44:53.420 --> 00:44:58.420
as well as existing reports
on the space oppression

00:44:58.480 --> 00:45:01.080
of the civic space, other state leaders

00:45:01.080 --> 00:45:05.020
have also combined like these strategies

00:45:05.020 --> 00:45:07.190
either in terms of framing
protesters for violence

00:45:07.190 --> 00:45:10.860
or inciting protestor
toward committing violence

00:45:10.860 --> 00:45:12.830
toward civilian targets,

00:45:12.830 --> 00:45:15.250
they will also combine decided strategies

00:45:15.250 --> 00:45:19.520
in order to provoke anti
protestor's hostility, right?

00:45:19.520 --> 00:45:22.520
So in this table, you can find
like some of those examples

00:45:22.520 --> 00:45:26.090
where state leaders across
Asia, Europe and Africa,

00:45:26.090 --> 00:45:29.550
and the Americas have simultaneously
spread this information

00:45:29.550 --> 00:45:33.410
both publicly and covertly and
going one step further like

00:45:33.410 --> 00:45:36.910
to what a number of pocketed
movements, political elites

00:45:36.910 --> 00:45:41.520
in the US, UK and Mexico,
not only actively vilified

00:45:41.520 --> 00:45:45.080
the protesters but also
employed Asians provocateurs

00:45:45.080 --> 00:45:49.800
who urge funded and also armed
protesters viling sabotage

00:45:49.800 --> 00:45:50.633
as well.

00:45:53.620 --> 00:45:56.720
So, therefore, right, like as we can see

00:45:56.720 --> 00:46:01.020
these very familiar
strategies being employed

00:46:01.020 --> 00:46:05.030
in different political regime context

00:46:05.030 --> 00:46:07.513
and also in the current
context of Myanmar,

00:46:08.440 --> 00:46:13.440
I hope that the findings
that I have presented today,

00:46:14.070 --> 00:46:16.890
like based on my research
on the case of Myanmar

00:46:16.890 --> 00:46:21.890
between 1988 and 2010 could
also offer some insights

00:46:22.160 --> 00:46:27.160
and also some relevant
perspectives to help us understand

00:46:30.568 --> 00:46:35.440
the implications of these
strategies in the case

00:46:35.440 --> 00:46:37.990
of the post-Myanmar and in other cases

00:46:37.990 --> 00:46:39.240
around the world as well.

00:46:40.660 --> 00:46:45.450
Yeah, so in conclusion, by
conducting the analysis like

00:46:45.450 --> 00:46:47.617
on the emergence of civilian hostility

00:46:47.617 --> 00:46:50.823
toward pro-democracy protests
in the Myanmar context,

00:46:52.128 --> 00:46:56.410
I would say that like
my research right now

00:46:56.410 --> 00:47:00.297
it compliments the current
literature on the 1988

00:47:00.297 --> 00:47:04.770
and 2007 events by presenting
a more comprehensive picture

00:47:04.770 --> 00:47:07.660
of an unimportant yet
largely neglected component

00:47:07.660 --> 00:47:11.660
of the state suppression
strategies to what dissent.

00:47:11.660 --> 00:47:14.470
And by illustrating these
mechanisms I also hope

00:47:14.470 --> 00:47:17.460
to contribute to the
Burma studies literature

00:47:17.460 --> 00:47:21.050
by refuting the claims
that the more participants

00:47:21.050 --> 00:47:24.150
in 1988 were exceptional

00:47:24.150 --> 00:47:29.150
or were particularly quote
unquote savage, right,

00:47:29.610 --> 00:47:31.380
like in any sense.

00:47:31.380 --> 00:47:33.500
They did not automatically become violent

00:47:33.500 --> 00:47:36.460
when there were no
functioning state institutions

00:47:36.460 --> 00:47:41.460
and as the Myanmar junta and
some scholars have claimed

00:47:41.990 --> 00:47:45.190
on the other hand like most
people they were disgusted

00:47:45.190 --> 00:47:48.220
and they were traumatized
by the public executions

00:47:48.220 --> 00:47:50.803
of the criminal suspects.

00:47:51.700 --> 00:47:54.990
And the strike committee members
moreover they were mainly

00:47:54.990 --> 00:47:58.030
under the influence of
the state incitement

00:47:58.030 --> 00:48:01.820
when they engage in such
types of collective assault.

00:48:01.820 --> 00:48:05.170
And my study is also mixed
contributions here toward

00:48:05.170 --> 00:48:07.840
the social movement and the
authoritarianism literature

00:48:07.840 --> 00:48:08.700
as well.

00:48:08.700 --> 00:48:09.840
So first it highlights

00:48:09.840 --> 00:48:11.860
the authoritarian
regime's deceptive tactics

00:48:11.860 --> 00:48:14.470
as an important part in their toolbox

00:48:14.470 --> 00:48:16.900
to manufacture a hostile environments

00:48:16.900 --> 00:48:20.570
for contentious actors and in
order to suppress the sense.

00:48:20.570 --> 00:48:23.940
And this would also deepen our
understanding of mechanisms

00:48:23.940 --> 00:48:26.190
for authoritative
survival, like, especially

00:48:26.190 --> 00:48:28.680
during critical times of public uprisings

00:48:28.680 --> 00:48:32.603
beyond the conventional models
of cooptation and repression.

00:48:34.035 --> 00:48:37.970
And I propose that this
finding can serve as a unique

00:48:37.970 --> 00:48:41.330
and valuable evidence for
advocacy against the suppression

00:48:41.330 --> 00:48:44.570
of civic space as human rights
activists and journalists,

00:48:44.570 --> 00:48:48.900
they might expose the covert
strategies and raise awareness

00:48:48.900 --> 00:48:51.850
among the general public
and international community

00:48:51.850 --> 00:48:54.210
in order to deter the state leaders

00:48:54.210 --> 00:48:57.150
from employing these
tactics in the future.

00:48:57.150 --> 00:49:00.600
And in another part of my
dissertation I also find

00:49:00.600 --> 00:49:05.110
that activists might also
neutralize these face strategies

00:49:05.110 --> 00:49:08.670
by actively debunking the
state this information

00:49:09.890 --> 00:49:14.740
by think vigilant to the
incitement to violence

00:49:14.740 --> 00:49:19.740
and also by coordinating
collective support,

00:49:19.910 --> 00:49:23.470
like from the general
public to what protesters.

00:49:23.470 --> 00:49:27.690
And the resulting support and
protection by the civilians

00:49:27.690 --> 00:49:31.010
in contrast to hostile
disruption would increase

00:49:31.010 --> 00:49:33.790
the activist survival and
commitment to the movement

00:49:33.790 --> 00:49:35.530
